1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
【来源信息，非音频原话】
频道：The Diary Of A CEO（音频文件名及 YouTube 节目页）
受访者：James Talarico（YouTube 节目页）
视频来源：https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FpIN6tYC5XM
以上为来源说明，不代表已逐句识别说话人或核实观点归属。
【转录正文】
For one of our own residents to become the world's first trillionaire,

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:05,700
while one in five Texas children live in poverty,

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:07,520
to me that is a spiritual sickness.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:11,340
Right now, it's three men who now own more wealth than the entire middle class.

5
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:13,280
As someone who cares about the people in my state,

6
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:14,420
that's not the future I want.

7
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,240
We have to have a government of, by, and for the people,

8
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:18,720
and we have to clean up the corruption in our political system.

9
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,200
Is the Texas governor corrupt?

10
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:20,780
Yes.

11
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:22,240
That's a strong thing to say.

12
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:24,200
Well, I'm an eighth generation Texan.

13
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,460
I was born to a single mother, a preacher's daughter.

14
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,520
She worked overtime to provide for me.

15
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,080
My birth father became abusive,

16
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,080
so she packed all our stuff into her little Ford Escort.

17
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,340
We found a little apartment.

18
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:37,460
There wasn't room for a nursery, so I slept in a crib in her closet.

19
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,180
And that fighting spirit, I feel like she passed that along to me.

20
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:42,680
But also, faith has really been the foundation of my life.

21
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:43,720
It's why I ran for office,

22
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:46,380
and I am trying to love my neighbor through public policy.

23
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:52,560
You're on the verge of potentially becoming the first Democratic senator in Texas since 1988.

24
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:54,420
So change is what we need most right now,

25
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:57,480
because everywhere I go, people are struggling to survive,

26
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,500
struggling to afford,

27
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:00,900
and people are working harder than they've ever worked.

28
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:04,540
And it's because the system itself has been rigged against working people.

29
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:05,620
We don't have capitalism.

30
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:06,980
We have crony capitalism.

31
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,000
Why did the Democrats lose to Trump, in your view?

32
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:12,840
I talked with a lot of Texans who thought he could get the economy back on track.

33
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,600
They thought he was going to lower costs, end the forever wars, release the Epstein files.

34
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:19,900
But more than a year into this administration, those promises have not been kept.

35
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:23,660
But he was right that the Biden administration failed us on our southern border.

36
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,700
So what would you do about that, though?

37
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:27,680
And how do you reverse this change?

38
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:28,500
And do you have an opinion?

39
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:30,680
And do you have an opinion on who you think should be president in the next cycle?

40
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,880
We should start with Donald Trump.

41
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:36,240
Here is a strange fact.

42
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:40,880
Most of the people watching this right now, about 58% of you, aren't yet subscribed to this channel.

43
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:43,240
Statistically, that is probably you.

44
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,560
So could I ask you a small favor?

45
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:47,840
If this channel has ever given you any value at all,

46
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:49,960
please could you do me a favor and hit the subscribe button?

47
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:51,040
It costs nothing.

48
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:52,520
It helps us more than you could know.

49
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:54,280
And the bigger the channel gets, as you've seen,

50
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,400
the more we can invest in the guests and the production.

51
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:57,400
So thank you so much.

52
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:58,400
And I hope you enjoy this video.

53
00:01:58,600 --> 00:01:59,600
And I'll see you in the next episode.

54
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,800
James Tallarico.

55
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
If the elections go your way this November,

56
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:14,200
you're on the verge of potentially making history by becoming the first Democratic senator in Texas history,

57
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:20,400
which would be the first time, I think, since 1988, that a Democrat has won in Texas.

58
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:25,600
And also, people tell me that you might be president someday.

59
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,400
Because you have a lot of the attributes.

60
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:34,100
That people think it would take a Democratic candidate to become president of the United States.

61
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:37,700
So really, I just want to figure out who you are and what you stand for and where you've come from.

62
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:42,500
What is that early context that I need to understand to understand the man that sits in front of me today?

63
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:45,100
Yeah, well, I'm an eighth generation Texan.

64
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,700
So my family's been in the state since it was Mexico.

65
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:54,700
Was born to a single mother who was a preacher's daughter from South Texas,

66
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:57,500
who struck it on her own when she was 19 years old,

67
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:04,600
moved up to the big city, Austin, Texas, and worked overtime to provide for me.

68
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:08,300
She left a dangerous situation to provide a better life for me.

69
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:09,500
Yep, this is her. Yeah.

70
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:12,700
She showed me what it means to stand up to bullies.

71
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:16,400
She showed me what it means to fight for the people that you love.

72
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:18,400
She was always making time for service.

73
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:19,600
She worked a full-time job.

74
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:27,400
She had very limited time, but she always carved out time to fight for the causes that she cared about,

75
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,500
whether it was at my school or at the church or in the community.

76
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:39,500
And so my service, my public service is inspired by her and I would not be here without her.

77
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:41,500
You said she taught you to stand up to bullies.

78
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:42,500
Yeah.

79
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,500
She was in an abusive relationship with your biological father.

80
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:47,500
Yes, she met my birth father.

81
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:49,500
He was 21 years old.

82
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:53,500
He was a high school dropout and he had a drinking problem.

83
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:57,300
And when he drank, he...

84
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,500
became violent, became abusive.

85
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:06,700
And one night shortly after I was born, he got physical again.

86
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:13,900
And that night, her love for me rose to meet that abuse from him.

87
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:20,100
And she packed all our stuff into her little Ford Escort and she drove me to the hotel where she worked,

88
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:26,500
begged the manager to let us stay in one of the rooms until we found a little apartment in East Austin.

89
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,600
This photo is taken in that apartment.

90
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:32,800
There wasn't room for a nursery, so I slept in a crib in her closet.

91
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,600
And she always says that she was very proud of that closet.

92
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:43,800
Like she decorated it with toys and pictures and that fighting spirit, I feel like she passed that along to me.

93
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,800
And whether it was in the classroom or in the state legislature, like I have tried to fight for the people I love in the way that she fought for me.

94
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:53,800
How do you feel?

95
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,600
How do you feel about him?

96
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:02,400
Well, you know, I remember when I was growing up, I felt a lot of anger toward him.

97
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:08,400
I feel like as I've gotten older, I've tried to appreciate that the things that got him to that point.

98
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:13,400
My mom would tell me that he didn't know the months in order.

99
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:18,600
And so she thinks that maybe he had a learning disability that went undiagnosed.

100
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:25,500
And things are very different now and we're able to, I know as a former teacher, that we're able to catch these disabilities.

101
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:29,900
And serve our students with special needs, but it sounds like he didn't get that.

102
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:33,300
And that can derail someone and it can lead to substance abuse.

103
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:39,400
It can lead to the bottle and then that can lead to violence and abuse.

104
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:46,900
And so I try as hard as it is to recognize that I'm the person I am because of him and my mom.

105
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:48,600
And that's a hard thing to wrestle with.

106
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:55,400
I think a lot of people who've had abuse in their family struggle with the fact that they come from an abuser.

107
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:00,700
And I've tried to have more compassion for it as I've gotten older.

108
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:04,100
Your mom then meets Mark Tallarico, who's your adoptive father.

109
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:05,100
That's right.

110
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:07,300
And he's the man that's ultimately raised you?

111
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:08,500
Absolutely, yeah.

112
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:09,500
Very different.

113
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:10,700
Yes, very different.

114
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:11,700
Than your birth father?

115
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:12,900
Yeah.

116
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:14,900
What role has he played in your life?

117
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:21,100
Yeah, you know, I've said before that the luckiest thing that ever happened to me in my whole life was meeting Mark Tallarico.

118
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:23,100
And I certainly would not be on this podcast.

119
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:24,300
He gave me his last name.

120
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:25,500
He raised me as his own.

121
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:36,700
He is probably the best man that I know, honest, and cares about other people, does what's right when no one is watching.

122
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:42,300
And he has certainly shaped me into the man that I am today.

123
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:46,500
If I'd asked you at 16 years old, James, if I said, what do you want to be when you're older, what would you have said?

124
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:54,300
I had always been interested in education and being a teacher, particularly around social studies and history.

125
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:59,100
My history teachers, when I was growing up, they're the ones who kept me on the straight and narrow.

126
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:00,400
They got me interested in school.

127
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:08,600
I was not the best student before then, and I struggled, you know, to behave in class and be a good student.

128
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:16,300
And I feel like it was my passion for social studies, for history, for reading that, you know, ultimately kept me on that track.

129
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:23,200
And so from that point, I did want to go into being some kind of a teacher or a professor so I could do that for other people.

130
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:27,200
I read that in high school, you were voted most likely to become president.

131
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:29,700
I think so, yeah.

132
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:33,300
When I was in school, I took on leadership roles.

133
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:38,500
I was the president of our choir and the captain of our debate team.

134
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:41,500
And so I think people, you know, saw that.

135
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:44,900
Are you interested in being president someday?

136
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:46,400
I don't think so.

137
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:51,100
I struggled a lot with the decision to run for the US Senate.

138
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,100
I had considered getting out of it.

139
00:07:53,300 --> 00:08:03,300
I had considered getting out of politics, I guess, as it was last summer after my fourth term in the legislature was coming to a close.

140
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:09,500
I finished the legislative session in May of last year, and I was really proud of the work that I had done in the legislature.

141
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:12,700
But it is difficult work, being in politics.

142
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:18,900
And so you may know that I also made the decision to go to seminary.

143
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:20,100
Which is Bible school?

144
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:23,100
Yes, studying to become a minister like my granddad.

145
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:26,300
It was something that I always kind of had in the back of my mind.

146
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:35,300
And I remember meeting with my pastor, who baptized me when I was two years old, has been my pastor since I was a little kid.

147
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:40,500
And he told me that a campaign can also be a form of ministry.

148
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:49,100
If you're giving people hope, if you're bringing people together, if you're helping folks see the image of God in each other and in themselves.

149
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:52,100
You can do that in a political context as well.

150
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:53,200
And I never really thought about that.

151
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,400
I kind of kept these things felt very distinct to me.

152
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:06,100
And he, of course, as a pastor, was helpful in getting me to reconcile these two vocations and see them as potentially one in the same.

153
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:13,700
Given the state of our politics and our civic discourse, like the campaign that we're building in Texas is giving people hope.

154
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:16,400
It's bringing people into the process who haven't been engaged before.

155
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:22,000
It's bringing people together across all the many divisions in our communities.

156
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:28,900
And at its best moments, it does feel like that we're doing something really, really special and important.

157
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:32,700
Why would that exclude you from someday also pursuing the role of presidency?

158
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:34,500
It may not.

159
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:39,500
I guess what I'm saying is that I think there's a problem with people who are in politics for too long.

160
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:47,200
I've already said that I've that I'll only be serving two terms if I'm blessed to get the opportunity to serve for two terms.

161
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:51,200
Because I do feel like when people hang on to these positions.

162
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:57,700
Or are trying to reach for the next position, then everything becomes about self-preservation.

163
00:09:57,900 --> 00:10:01,900
It becomes about accumulating power rather than serving people.

164
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:03,500
And those are temptations, right?

165
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:06,000
For every elected official between those two goals.

166
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:16,900
And from what I've seen being in this now for eight years is the longer you're in this, the more you kind of get the office and your identity become kind of merged into one thing, right?

167
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:21,100
Where being a senator or being a state rep, which I am now, if you're doing it for a really long time.

168
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:22,500
It becomes who you are.

169
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:24,700
So it's no longer a service.

170
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:28,900
It is your identity and you do whatever you can to protect that, right?

171
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:31,500
Which means selling out your values.

172
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:33,800
It means being susceptible to corruption.

173
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,200
And so I guess starting out, I'm trying to like always remember that this is a service.

174
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:47,500
And I think we need more elected officials who have other interests besides politics and government and legislating.

175
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:50,700
There are other things that I want to do and there are other ways I want to serve.

176
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:54,800
I want to have a well-balanced life, I guess.

177
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,300
And so I want to do this because I think it's important right now.

178
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:07,700
But then I want to like, I want to give the reins over to someone else, someone who has fresh energy and new ideas and a different perspective.

179
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:09,500
And I think that's how this is supposed to work.

180
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:13,200
Could you see yourself running someday for the highest office in the land, president?

181
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:14,000
No.

182
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:14,700
You couldn't?

183
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:16,100
No, I really don't.

184
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:17,300
I'm surprised I'm doing this.

185
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:20,100
I'm running for the US Senate.

186
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:22,800
And I feel like there's so much.

187
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:28,900
I mean, I'm traveling the whole state of Texas, which is a giant state, a diverse state, complex state.

188
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:32,000
And I'm constantly learning about the things I don't know.

189
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:32,600
Right.

190
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:41,900
And I feel like I'm just every day on the campaign trail cramming for a big test in January of next year to be the senator of this state.

191
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:46,500
And I've already seen over the course of this campaign how difficult it is.

192
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:46,900
Right.

193
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:48,300
And the attacks that come your way.

194
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:49,700
The attacks on people in your life.

195
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:59,900
Like your family, my girlfriend, people who didn't sign up to be in office or run for office are also on the receiving end of these attacks.

196
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:04,100
And so I can't imagine doing this for longer than two terms.

197
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:12,700
Do you think pressure and attacks are a reason not to do something that is worthy?

198
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:14,700
You know, I'm just wondering.

199
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:15,400
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

200
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:17,600
Because even as a podcaster, you get lots of pressure.

201
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:18,800
It's a different type of pressure.

202
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:19,700
No.

203
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:22,000
You get lots of pressure, lots of attacks, lots of feedback, et cetera.

204
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:25,400
But at the end of it, like, I think I love what I get to do.

205
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:27,300
And I really hope that people benefit from it.

206
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:29,900
So to me, that's reason enough to continue to do it.

207
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:32,500
Really irrespective of the pressure and the attacks.

208
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:33,500
I think that's true.

209
00:12:33,700 --> 00:12:35,500
But I think there are lots of people who want to be president.

210
00:12:35,700 --> 00:12:39,100
And so, like, we should give those people a shot if that's the job they want.

211
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:45,500
I honestly think that my skill set that I've developed is better suited for a legislative position.

212
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:47,300
What's a legislative position for us Brits?

213
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:48,100
Oh, yeah, yeah.

214
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:50,000
So, like, right now, I'm a state.

215
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:53,000
I grew up in Texas, so I've served in the state house in the lower chamber.

216
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,400
I would be, God willing, after this election, serving in the U.S.

217
00:12:57,600 --> 00:13:00,400
Senate, which means I'm one member of a larger team of people.

218
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:03,000
I am trying to build coalitions.

219
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:05,700
I'm trying to bring people over to my side.

220
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:10,400
I'm trying to change hearts and minds in a body.

221
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:11,700
And I feel like I'm really good at that.

222
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:17,200
I mean, in the state house in Texas, even as a Democrat in a Republican-dominated body,

223
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:20,000
I've passed 60 bipartisan bills.

224
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:26,000
I've passed a lot of bills that are not just, like, symbolic, you know, superficial pieces of legislation.

225
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:32,200
Like, these are major bills on housing and healthcare and early childhood education, teacher pay, property taxes,

226
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:35,200
like, the things that are really affecting my neighbors.

227
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:36,400
And that is a skill set.

228
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:41,600
Some people are better suited as, you know, as an executive making decisions on their own.

229
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:48,000
And some people are better, you know, working as part of a larger team to do something ambitious and big.

230
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:50,000
You must have an opinion on them.

231
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:52,900
I think it might be, you know, because we head towards 2028.

232
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:58,100
If you look at the polling data and what's going on geopolitically with Iran and lots of other issues like AI,

233
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:03,600
it appears that there's a, you know, the polls are becoming a little bit more favorable for the Democrats.

234
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:07,300
Do you have an opinion on who you think should be president in the next cycle?

235
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:11,300
Kamala's, there's Gavin's, there's AOC's, there's, I don't know, Bernie's probably going to run again.

236
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:14,200
Pete Buttigieg's probably going to run again.

237
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:19,900
Yeah. I mean, I don't, I don't, given that I worked at the state level, I don't know a lot of these people personally.

238
00:14:20,100 --> 00:14:27,900
I honestly think a lot of the people that you just named, you know, have a lot of potential to be, to be good leaders and to deliver for this country.

239
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:31,400
And honestly, I'll, you know, change is what we need most right now.

240
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:36,400
So I'm excited for anyone who, who's going to bring some, some change to this political system.

241
00:14:36,600 --> 00:14:45,900
The only thing I would say is what I'm going to be looking for in, in, in the candidates as they start bubbling up is like, who's talking about systemic reform?

242
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:47,700
There's lots of issues that I care about.

243
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:49,500
Education, healthcare.

244
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:59,500
Housing, all things that I care about, but I've done this now for eight years and I've seen that the system itself is what's preventing us from making progress on all of those important issues.

245
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:18,100
And so when I'm kind of thinking about my criteria for evaluating who I'm going to vote for or who I'm going to support, it's going to be who is, who is thinking and talking about comprehensive political reform, including getting big money out of our politics, banning gerrymandering, overhauling the Supreme Court.

246
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:19,800
All these things that feel like.

247
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,300
Inside baseball that feel like process.

248
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:29,100
It has to be the top priority because if we don't fix the system itself, we're not going to make progress on all the issues we care about.

249
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:32,700
There's been this really interesting rise in democratic socialism.

250
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:32,900
Yeah.

251
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:36,200
Over the last, over the last year or so.

252
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:36,500
Yeah.

253
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:44,600
Obviously, Mayor Mondani has been elected to office and, you know, people like AOC and just the broader democratic socialism narrative has been really popular.

254
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:46,000
What's your perspective on that?

255
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,800
Cause it, I think of like the, the Democrats here that I think of democratic socialism.

256
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:56,400
Big as being slightly different from sort of typical, more centrist, democratic philosophy.

257
00:15:57,100 --> 00:16:01,400
I certainly understand where the, where the anger is coming from.

258
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:02,900
I hear it everywhere.

259
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:04,200
I go across the state of Texas.

260
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:04,800
Anger.

261
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:05,400
Yeah.

262
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:11,900
I mean, everywhere I go, people are, are struggling to survive in this economy.

263
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:17,400
And it's not like people are buying nice clothes or nice cars or nice vacations.

264
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:19,800
Like they're struggling to afford necessity.

265
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:19,900
Yeah.

266
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:20,000
Yeah.

267
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:30,400
They're struggling to afford necessities, groceries and gas and utilities, prescription drugs, that the things that you, that you need to survive every day, and people are working harder than they've ever worked, right?

268
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:35,000
Some of them are working multiple jobs and they can't make those ends meet.

269
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:39,400
And this is especially pronounced among young people.

270
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:49,900
In my experience, my fellow millennials, but also Gen Z, because we were promised that if we worked hard, if we played by the rules, if we went to school, if we studied.

271
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:51,300
That we could live the American dream.

272
00:16:52,100 --> 00:16:57,800
And that was a lie because it is way too expensive to buy a home.

273
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:01,900
It's way too expensive to, to start a family, to have kids.

274
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:04,800
It's too expensive to retire comfortably.

275
00:17:04,900 --> 00:17:09,000
And it's because the system itself has been rigged against working people.

276
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:12,100
And I would say, I would go back to the 1970s.

277
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:18,900
In fact, you can, you can pinpoint this one document called the Powell memo, 1971.

278
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:19,800
He was a corporate lawyer.

279
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:23,100
Who later got appointed to the Supreme court.

280
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:35,500
But if you read this memo, it's basically a blueprint for how corporate interests can start to dismantle the great society and the new deal, which had come the decades before.

281
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:49,500
And so there was a feeling that among corporate America, that there needed to be a, a strategic plan, a political campaign to protect their power in a mass.

282
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:50,400
More power.

283
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:54,400
And so the, the blueprint says that corporate interests are going to unite.

284
00:17:54,700 --> 00:18:05,000
They're going to spend massive amounts of money in our politics, buying politicians in both parties, elevate corporate voices in media, appoint sympathetic judges.

285
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:13,800
It goes through kind of a, a whole comprehensive strategy for how to reverse the gains made in the new deal and the great society.

286
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:18,700
And, and you start to see starting in the late seventies, early eighties.

287
00:18:19,300 --> 00:18:21,100
Trickle down economics, right.

288
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:25,500
Of cutting taxes for the people at the very top and forgetting about the rest of us.

289
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,900
And that has really been the story of the last 50 years in this country, the last five decades.

290
00:18:30,300 --> 00:18:41,200
And that's a major change from what we saw in the middle of the 20th century, when our government invested in, in education and healthcare in homes and highways.

291
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:43,000
Like we, we grew together.

292
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,300
We had the largest, strongest middle class in the world in the 1950s and 1960s.

293
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:48,600
And now that.

294
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:53,000
That middle class is disappearing all over Texas, all over this country.

295
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:55,000
And there's a lot of anger about that.

296
00:18:55,300 --> 00:18:58,400
And it's not just limited to Democrats or democratic socialists.

297
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:00,800
It really spans the political spectrum.

298
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:07,600
And people are looking for, they're looking for leaders who are going to take on that broken corrupt system.

299
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:11,500
Not nibble around the edges, not incremental improvements.

300
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:18,200
People are looking for wholesale change and, and their, their right to, to want that change.

301
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:24,800
Because it is possible to build an economy that rewards work instead of wealth.

302
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:26,000
That's very possible.

303
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:31,500
I mean, the economy is just a collective, a collection of choices that we make, particularly policy choices.

304
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:35,600
And we can make different choices than the ones that we've made for the last five decades.

305
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:37,100
Trickle-down economics.

306
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:39,500
In my view is not a theory.

307
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:40,200
It's theft.

308
00:19:41,100 --> 00:19:47,400
In 1970, middle-class households held 62% of all US aggregate income while upper income households held 29%.

309
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:48,100
29% today.

310
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:50,000
Today, those roles have flipped.

311
00:19:50,300 --> 00:19:56,700
Upper income households capture over 850% of total US income compared to just 42% held by the middle class.

312
00:19:56,900 --> 00:20:06,600
If we look at lower income families, there's been a 5% increase since the 1970s in the sort of cohort of people living in the sort of low income category.

313
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:13,700
The middle income category has reduced by 10% and the upper income class has increased by 5%.

314
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:17,200
So the direction of travel is that there's less people in the middle class.

315
00:20:17,200 --> 00:20:17,900
There's more people in the lower class.

316
00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:21,400
There's more people in the lower income and there's more people in higher income.

317
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:21,800
Yeah.

318
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:23,900
How does one reverse that?

319
00:20:23,900 --> 00:20:33,000
Because you know, whatever the sort of economic system has been in the United States has resulted in innovation and GDP growth and productivity.

320
00:20:33,100 --> 00:20:35,100
How do you reverse this change?

321
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:39,800
Well, what I'm talking about is not a radical new vision for the economy.

322
00:20:39,800 --> 00:20:46,400
I'm talking about how do we get back to the economy of the 1950s and 60s where we grew together as a nation.

323
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:47,700
And in fact, the,

324
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:48,900
the productivity,

325
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:54,600
the innovation was I think more significant when we were growing together.

326
00:20:54,600 --> 00:21:03,000
I think the math and the past make it very clear that a country that invests in its people grows faster than a country that abandons them.

327
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:04,300
And so specifically,

328
00:21:04,300 --> 00:21:05,300
what am I talking about?

329
00:21:05,600 --> 00:21:07,800
I'm talking about raising the minimum wage,

330
00:21:08,500 --> 00:21:13,800
expanding overtime pay so that workers can actually benefit from the wealth they create.

331
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:17,000
You know, capitalism was an innovation.

332
00:21:17,100 --> 00:21:17,400
We've,

333
00:21:17,400 --> 00:21:18,800
I think we forget that now.

334
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:21,100
The idea that you should be rewarded,

335
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:24,300
that you should be compensated for your time,

336
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:24,900
for your work.

337
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:26,900
That was a revolutionary idea.

338
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:29,100
And that's not what we have today.

339
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:30,800
We don't have capitalism,

340
00:21:30,900 --> 00:21:31,900
at least not in this country.

341
00:21:32,600 --> 00:21:33,900
We have crony capitalism.

342
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:34,800
What is that?

343
00:21:35,100 --> 00:21:40,100
Where the gains in this economy go to those who can afford to buy access,

344
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:43,400
who can afford to buy politicians for control.

345
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:44,100
Dr.

346
00:21:44,100 --> 00:21:45,100
King said something similar.

347
00:21:45,100 --> 00:21:45,600
He said,

348
00:21:45,600 --> 00:21:50,300
we have socialism for the rich and we have rugged individualism for the poor.

349
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:52,200
You think about the bailout,

350
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:52,700
right?

351
00:21:52,700 --> 00:21:54,800
And the Great Recession,

352
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:55,200
right?

353
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:58,500
When a lot of the people on Wall Street who got us into that mess,

354
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:00,500
they got golden parachutes,

355
00:22:00,600 --> 00:22:00,800
right?

356
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:02,600
They got taken care of by the government.

357
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:04,700
But you got single moms out there.

358
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:07,000
You got construction workers.

359
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,400
You got nurses and teachers who are barely surviving in this economy.

360
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:12,900
And there's no bailout for them,

361
00:22:13,300 --> 00:22:13,500
right?

362
00:22:13,500 --> 00:22:15,200
It's rugged individualism for them.

363
00:22:15,800 --> 00:22:19,800
And so my only point is that we can create an economy where we all grow

364
00:22:19,800 --> 00:22:20,300
together,

365
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:24,500
where corporations have a broader definition of shareholder,

366
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:25,400
right?

367
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:27,300
Because you think about GE,

368
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:28,800
you think about GM,

369
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:29,500
Ford,

370
00:22:29,500 --> 00:22:32,000
these great American companies from the 20th century,

371
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:37,200
they believed that shareholders included their workers,

372
00:22:37,500 --> 00:22:38,800
the families of their workers,

373
00:22:38,800 --> 00:22:40,900
the communities in which they operated.

374
00:22:41,300 --> 00:22:42,800
So they felt an obligation,

375
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:44,800
a corporate ethos that

376
00:22:44,800 --> 00:22:47,400
was about much more than just their bottom line.

377
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,100
And so that's what I'm trying to get us back to.

378
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:55,000
I'm trying to get us back to a point where we are investing in our people and

379
00:22:55,100 --> 00:22:55,700
we have growth,

380
00:22:55,700 --> 00:22:56,500
but it's shared growth.

381
00:22:56,500 --> 00:22:57,200
We have competition,

382
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:58,200
but it's fair competition,

383
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:01,300
not monopolies and consolidation like we see today.

384
00:23:01,900 --> 00:23:05,600
So I guess what I'm trying to get us back to is old school capitalism,

385
00:23:05,800 --> 00:23:06,500
where again,

386
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:11,200
people are compensated for their work and it's not a system that benefits the wealthy

387
00:23:11,200 --> 00:23:12,300
and the well-connected only.

388
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:14,700
And how do you think you can do that?

389
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:16,000
From a Senate position,

390
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:17,300
if you are to win in Texas?

391
00:23:17,300 --> 00:23:17,700
Yeah.

392
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:23,500
So we talked about a few policies of minimum wage and overtime pay,

393
00:23:23,700 --> 00:23:26,800
but I also think we have to rebuild labor unions in this country.

394
00:23:26,800 --> 00:23:32,300
You can almost track the destruction of the middle class with the destruction of organized labor.

395
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:34,200
And this is not a government solution,

396
00:23:34,200 --> 00:23:34,400
really.

397
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:40,800
This is about empowering workers in the workplace to be able to negotiate with their bosses for better pay,

398
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:41,900
for better benefits,

399
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:43,600
for better working conditions.

400
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:44,600
I think we've got to do,

401
00:23:44,700 --> 00:23:47,300
more to tackle the debt crisis,

402
00:23:47,300 --> 00:23:49,200
both personal household debt,

403
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:51,700
which reached an all-time high in the first quarter of this year,

404
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:53,500
whether it's medical debt,

405
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:54,900
whether it's student debt,

406
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:56,000
credit card debt,

407
00:23:56,700 --> 00:23:58,800
and we should also talk about the national debt,

408
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:02,400
because both are moral emergencies,

409
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:03,300
in my opinion.

410
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:08,400
I also think we got to do more on supporting families and children.

411
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:10,000
I think investing in education,

412
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:13,300
the GI Bill was a game changer in the middle of the 20th century,

413
00:24:13,300 --> 00:24:14,600
and it's what helped build that middle class.

414
00:24:14,700 --> 00:24:22,700
We haven't seen an investment in higher education at that scale since the GI Bill.

415
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:23,200
You know,

416
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:25,000
I think we got to expand the child tax credit,

417
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:28,200
which cut child poverty in half just a few years ago,

418
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:29,700
and then it was allowed to expire.

419
00:24:30,200 --> 00:24:32,900
New families who are having kids,

420
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:35,900
like they need extra support if they're going to live that American dream,

421
00:24:35,900 --> 00:24:36,700
and we can do that.

422
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:44,500
And we can do all of this if we just were willing to tax the people at the very top and make them pay their fair share in taxes.

423
00:24:44,700 --> 00:24:50,800
Is that easier said than done in light of the forces that are opposed to what you've just said?

424
00:24:51,100 --> 00:24:55,000
There are big companies and individuals lobbying against what you've just said.

425
00:24:55,300 --> 00:24:56,100
And that's the point.

426
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:59,100
It is a broken political system,

427
00:24:59,100 --> 00:25:00,900
which leads to a broken economic system.

428
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:04,000
That is the direction that I think this moves in.

429
00:25:04,200 --> 00:25:06,000
And it's why I said earlier,

430
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:09,300
I'm looking for comprehensive political reform first,

431
00:25:09,700 --> 00:25:12,800
because the only way to unrig the economy so that it benefits all of us again,

432
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:14,500
so that we're all growing together again,

433
00:25:14,500 --> 00:25:16,100
that work is rewarded,

434
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:19,700
is if you clean up the corruption in our political system,

435
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:21,900
where corporate special interests,

436
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:22,800
corporate PACs,

437
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:27,100
super PACs don't have the same influence in our government as they do now.

438
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:28,500
Because right now,

439
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:28,900
I mean,

440
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:29,800
you know,

441
00:25:29,800 --> 00:25:31,200
I think about in Texas,

442
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:36,500
there was an out-of-state billionaire from Pennsylvania who gave our governor $12 million,

443
00:25:36,900 --> 00:25:39,100
because we don't have campaign contribution limits in Texas.

444
00:25:40,500 --> 00:25:42,700
He gave our governor $12 million

445
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:43,700
to pass

446
00:25:43,700 --> 00:25:45,700
a private school voucher scam,

447
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:49,200
which is taking money out of our underfunded public schools in Texas.

448
00:25:49,500 --> 00:25:52,500
We rank 43rd in the nation in per student education funding.

449
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:57,800
It's giving those tax dollars to wealthy parents who already have their kids in private school.

450
00:25:58,300 --> 00:25:58,900
So it is a,

451
00:25:58,900 --> 00:26:04,100
it is literally a transfer of wealth from the bottom in the middle to the people at the very top,

452
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:07,600
all because of corruption in our political system.

453
00:26:08,200 --> 00:26:13,600
So I don't think we're going to unrig this economy until we get rid of corruption in our politics and in our government.

454
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:15,200
We have to have a government of,

455
00:26:15,200 --> 00:26:15,400
by,

456
00:26:15,400 --> 00:26:18,100
and for the people instead of a what we have now,

457
00:26:18,100 --> 00:26:19,800
which is a government of,

458
00:26:19,800 --> 00:26:20,100
by,

459
00:26:20,100 --> 00:26:21,600
and for corporate special interests.

460
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:23,400
Is the Texas governor corrupt?

461
00:26:23,700 --> 00:26:24,200
Yes.

462
00:26:25,700 --> 00:26:26,600
That's a strong,

463
00:26:26,600 --> 00:26:27,500
strong thing to say.

464
00:26:28,200 --> 00:26:28,500
Yeah.

465
00:26:28,500 --> 00:26:29,200
I mean,

466
