After the pandemic, and the rise of inflation, the increase in inflation was kind of like the straw that broke the camel's back. Right? You'd had 50 years of trickle down economics of stagnant wages for middle class and working class people, systematic attacks on education and healthcare. You had all of that happening, but you had cheap goods. So there was that felt like we were getting something right, my income's not going up. You know, I'm not able to get ahead, like maybe my grandparents were able to get ahead. It's hard to start a business. But at least things are cheap.

883
00:58:44,760 --> 00:59:14,740
And then when that changed, suddenly people realized that we are all getting screwed by this system. Because not only is our is our pay going down or staying flat, but now the cost of living has gone up. So you're getting squeezed from both ends. So when that happened, I think people were looking for someone who was going to help. And that's not an unreasonable thing to look for when you're hurting. And so people put their faith in, in President Trump, for good reasons, right? They thought he was going to lower costs, they thought he was going to, you know, lower costs.

884
00:59:14,760 --> 00:59:44,360
And in the forever wars that we were all sick and tired of, they thought he was going to release the Epstein files. They thought he was going to drain the swamp. I mean, I have members of my family who voted for President Trump, and people that I love. And those are the reasons they those are the reasons they voted for him. And those are pretty good reasons. I think, you know, more than a year into this administration, those promises have not been kept. Everything is now more expensive, largely because of tariffs. We have a new war in Iran.

885
00:59:44,760 --> 00:59:59,000
No one asked for it's costing us a billion dollars a day that's driving up gas prices. The Epstein files have still not fully been released. And the swamp is bigger than it's ever been before. And so people are feeling like they like they've been conned.

886
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:17,520
My job as a candidate, our job as a campaign is to extend an open hand to our neighbors who are disillusioned, who misplaced their trust in a leader, and invite them to join this movement that we're building in Texas, which is honestly bigger than Donald Trump.

887
01:00:17,640 --> 01:00:19,100
It's bigger than any one political party.

888
01:00:19,100 --> 01:00:21,800
It really is on the ground.

889
01:00:21,960 --> 01:00:28,760
It feels like a movement of working people across the spectrum who are ready to take on this broken, corrupt system.

890
01:00:28,760 --> 01:00:42,540
And, you know, God willing, as the next U.S. senator from Texas, I will have the opportunity to work with President Trump to try to deliver for my constituents.

891
01:00:42,940 --> 01:00:44,240
What do you think of him as a person?

892
01:00:45,040 --> 01:00:46,560
Well, I don't know him, so it's hard for me to judge.

893
01:00:46,720 --> 01:00:51,640
But, you know, I think all of us have strengths and weaknesses.

894
01:00:52,780 --> 01:00:54,940
I think the president does have clear strengths.

895
01:00:54,940 --> 01:00:58,120
I think he's able to communicate in a way that's clear.

896
01:00:58,320 --> 01:00:58,400
And.

897
01:00:58,760 --> 01:01:01,200
And concise and powerful to people.

898
01:01:01,700 --> 01:01:05,400
He is willing to take action when there's so much gridlock.

899
01:01:06,100 --> 01:01:09,960
He's willing to just kind of bust through walls to deliver for people.

900
01:01:10,180 --> 01:01:14,120
And he has a sense of humor, which I feel like is really missing in politics.

901
01:01:14,540 --> 01:01:16,200
He has a lighthearted way about him.

902
01:01:17,120 --> 01:01:19,040
Those are, I think, some of the redeeming qualities.

903
01:01:19,040 --> 01:01:28,720
I think all of us know his willingness to put his ego above everything else, his willingness to make chaotic decisions without much forethought.

904
01:01:28,760 --> 01:01:39,960
So I think he's a balanced person and often, and I've had some good conversations with him about his pension for corruption and self enrichment for himself and his family and his his desire to be popular and all politicians have a desire to be popular.

905
01:01:39,960 --> 01:01:47,560
But he does seem to place that above almost any other principle or conviction that he may have.

906
01:01:47,560 --> 01:01:55,020
And so, you know, I think I think it's important to consider both the the good and the bad of any leader and that includes the president.

907
01:01:55,500 --> 01:01:58,760
What about immigration that's one of the issues that is very rarely discussed in the United States.

908
01:01:58,760 --> 01:02:04,580
relevant to texas but also that frankly helped him get elected yeah by him talking about some

909
01:02:04,580 --> 01:02:09,820
of these stats around border crossings yeah in texas yeah and he was right about this the biden

910
01:02:09,820 --> 01:02:14,580
administration failed us on our southern border i remember talking with my colleagues in the state

911
01:02:14,580 --> 01:02:20,800
house who represented border communities and they talked about the utter chaos um on our border and

912
01:02:20,800 --> 01:02:24,980
our border communities in texas mean a lot to me my mom is from laredo which is on our texas mexico

913
01:02:24,980 --> 01:02:31,540
border and so this system was out of control in in the biden era and you see it on this map

914
01:02:31,540 --> 01:02:37,620
my worry is that we have replaced one extremism on immigration in the biden years with a new

915
01:02:37,620 --> 01:02:43,860
extremism on immigration with masked men and unmarked vehicles kidnapping people off the

916
01:02:43,860 --> 01:02:49,020
street deporting small business owners who have been here for years and and decades taking

917
01:02:49,020 --> 01:02:52,580
children as young as five years old and throwing them in detention centers

918
01:02:52,580 --> 01:02:54,960
so my sense

919
01:02:54,960 --> 01:03:00,800
is that most texans most americans want the same thing when it comes to immigration they want

920
01:03:00,800 --> 01:03:06,340
security and they want to welcome hard-working immigrants who want to live the american dream

921
01:03:06,340 --> 01:03:11,220
and in our politics those two things are pitted against each other again back to false choices

922
01:03:11,220 --> 01:03:18,580
those are not mutually exclusive you can have a secure border you can you can it is not racist

923
01:03:18,580 --> 01:03:24,180
to want to know who's coming into this country right you can do all that while also building

924
01:03:24,180 --> 01:03:24,940
more legal

925
01:03:24,940 --> 01:03:29,100
pathways for folks to come here the right way folks who want to start a business who want to

926
01:03:29,100 --> 01:03:33,920
contribute to this economy who want to live that american dream and make us richer and stronger in

927
01:03:33,920 --> 01:03:41,040
the process as immigrants have always done and so i'm trying to on the trail uphold both of those

928
01:03:41,040 --> 01:03:46,460
moral values safety which public safety is the most important thing the government does more

929
01:03:46,460 --> 01:03:50,960
important than public education more important than public health public safety is the most

930
01:03:50,960 --> 01:03:53,800
important thing and we are a nation of immigrants

931
01:03:53,800 --> 01:03:59,640
It is the lifeblood of our economy. It is what has made Texas and America so great.

932
01:04:00,320 --> 01:04:07,040
And so we can uphold both of those values at the same time and pass comprehensive immigration reform that can do both of those things simultaneously.

933
01:04:07,380 --> 01:04:15,500
What would you do about the issue of undocumented young people that have come here, that are in the country, that, you know, maybe were brought here by no fault of their own, but

934
01:04:15,540 --> 01:04:17,880
now find themselves in the U.S. without documents?

935
01:04:18,160 --> 01:04:22,780
Yeah, I taught a lot of DACA students, a lot of Dreamers in Room 112 at Rhodes Middle School

936
01:04:22,780 --> 01:04:24,260
on the west side of San Antonio.

937
01:04:24,920 --> 01:04:42,240
My students who were undocumented, they loved this country in a profound way. It feels like they understand something about America that many of our leaders have forgotten, that this is supposed to be the land of opportunity. This is supposed to be the place where dreams come true.

938
01:04:42,240 --> 01:05:05,280
And so I would be very interested as part of a comprehensive immigration reform package, which includes border security, to create a pathway to citizenship for undocumented folks who have been here for years, for decades, for kids who were brought over as kids, so they have an opportunity to come out of the shadows and fully join us as citizens of this country.

939
01:05:07,200 --> 01:05:10,120
This is something that I've made for you.

940
01:05:10,200 --> 01:05:12,200
I realize that the [name unclear] audience are strivers.

941
01:05:12,200 --> 01:05:16,340
Whether it's in business or health, we all have big goals that we want to accomplish.

942
01:05:16,840 --> 01:05:28,440
And one of the things I've learned is that when you aim at the big, big, big goal, it can feel incredibly psychologically uncomfortable because it's kind of like being stood at the foot of Mount Everest and looking upwards.

943
01:05:28,940 --> 01:05:33,800
The way to accomplish your goals is by breaking them down into tiny, small steps.

944
01:05:33,920 --> 01:05:35,680
And we call this in our team the 1%.

945
01:05:35,680 --> 01:05:39,960
And actually, this philosophy is highly responsible for much of our success here.

946
01:05:39,960 --> 01:05:49,920
So what we've done is that you at home can accomplish any big goal that you have is we've made these 1% diaries and we released these last year and they all sold out.

947
01:05:50,260 --> 01:05:56,220
So I asked my team over and over again to bring the diaries back, but also to introduce some new colors and to make some minor tweaks to the diary.

948
01:05:56,340 --> 01:06:00,760
So now we have a better range for you.

949
01:06:00,760 --> 01:06:09,800
So if you have a big goal in mind and you need a framework and a process and some motivation, then I highly recommend you get one of these diaries before they all sell out.

950
01:06:09,800 --> 01:06:13,380
Once again, and you can get yours at the diary.com.

951
01:06:13,580 --> 01:06:15,340
And if you want the link, the link is in the description below.

952
01:06:16,060 --> 01:06:18,360
There should be a button just down below here.

953
01:06:18,420 --> 01:06:20,740
And if it says subscribe, you're already subscribed.

954
01:06:20,860 --> 01:06:23,720
If it says subscriber, that means you're not yet.

955
01:06:23,960 --> 01:06:26,340
And if you're not subscribed, please could you do us a favor and hit that button?

956
01:06:26,420 --> 01:06:27,720
It helps the show more than you know.

957
01:06:28,160 --> 01:06:32,160
And according to the algorithm, you're someone that watches our show, but you haven't yet hit that button.

958
01:06:32,300 --> 01:06:32,920
Thank you so much.

959
01:06:34,060 --> 01:06:37,380
One of the issues that I know is going to be front of mind for a lot of people at the moment.

960
01:06:37,380 --> 01:06:39,780
And one of the issues I think that's going to result.

961
01:06:39,800 --> 01:06:45,180
In the Republican Party struggling in the midterms is this issue you mentioned earlier of Iran.

962
01:06:45,580 --> 01:06:45,680
Yeah.

963
01:06:45,720 --> 01:06:52,420
If you are a U.S. senator, you would be voting on foreign policy and war powers and military spending and things like that, including in the Middle East.

964
01:06:52,780 --> 01:06:56,920
So I wanted to get your perspective on what the hell is going on.

965
01:06:57,100 --> 01:06:58,020
That's a good question.

966
01:06:58,180 --> 01:07:00,060
How do you get out of this situation now?

967
01:07:00,100 --> 01:07:03,720
It seems like there's no golden bridge out of the situation.

968
01:07:04,080 --> 01:07:04,260
Yeah.

969
01:07:04,300 --> 01:07:07,180
I mean, we shouldn't have been in this situation to begin with.

970
01:07:07,220 --> 01:07:08,040
We should start with that.

971
01:07:08,040 --> 01:07:09,560
How did we get in this situation?

972
01:07:09,800 --> 01:07:31,980
Again, this is just from an outsider perspective, but it seems like after the operation in Venezuela, there was a hubris and overconfidence that toppling the leadership in Iran would be an easy thing and would be a quick fix to a problem that has long plagued the region and plagued the United States.

973
01:07:32,980 --> 01:07:36,620
And maybe a lack of foresight and consideration.

974
01:07:36,620 --> 01:07:39,500
I mean, it's not that hard to predict.

975
01:07:39,800 --> 01:07:45,160
That what Iran would do when they're cornered is take control of the Strait of Hormuz.

976
01:07:45,820 --> 01:07:50,700
In fact, previous administrations had played out these war games and had come to the same conclusion.

977
01:07:51,460 --> 01:07:52,220
So what do you do now?

978
01:07:52,580 --> 01:07:53,800
Because you're going to vote on this stuff.

979
01:07:54,220 --> 01:07:54,360
Yeah.

980
01:07:54,500 --> 01:07:56,860
I mean, one, I think we need checks and balances.

981
01:07:57,260 --> 01:08:09,780
Right now, we have something that the founders, I think, couldn't have envisioned, which is parties, political parties, factions is what the founders would have called them, coordinating across branches of our government.

982
01:08:10,360 --> 01:08:22,360
So instead of, you know, the ambitions of the legislative branch, checking the ambitions of the executive branch, you now have members of one party coordinating across those branches.

983
01:08:22,360 --> 01:08:27,740
And what that looks like now is a legislative branch, which is supposed to be the most important branch in our government, right?

984
01:08:27,800 --> 01:08:29,140
It's the first one listed in the Constitution.

985
01:08:29,700 --> 01:08:37,020
It has now become completely subservient to the executive branch, to the will of the president and his administration.

986
01:08:37,860 --> 01:08:39,580
And so, one, I think you have to restore.

987
01:08:39,720 --> 01:08:39,780
Yeah.

988
01:08:39,800 --> 01:08:43,560
And it doesn't have to be in a hostile, unproductive way.

989
01:08:44,400 --> 01:08:50,100
It can be in a healthy way where the legislative branch is asking questions about what is the military strategy?

990
01:08:50,260 --> 01:08:52,920
What are the military objectives that we're trying to accomplish?

991
01:08:53,020 --> 01:08:57,620
Because we've seen over the course of this disastrous war, those objectives change over time.

992
01:08:58,160 --> 01:08:58,800
But this is the thing.

993
01:08:58,900 --> 01:09:01,080
It doesn't appear that they know what the objectives are anymore.

994
01:09:01,080 --> 01:09:03,380
And I think this is key.

995
01:09:03,720 --> 01:09:09,720
If we cannot clearly articulate an objective, then it is the responsibility of the…

996
01:09:09,720 --> 01:09:21,700
of the legislative branch to intervene and to withhold funding for these operations until we can get a clear idea of why we are sending our brave young men and women into another forever war in the Middle East.

997
01:09:22,120 --> 01:09:26,120
I think, ultimately, how you untangle this is through regional partnerships.

998
01:09:26,800 --> 01:09:30,900
It has to involve – I mean, we kind of know the contours of what a deal would look like.

999
01:09:31,360 --> 01:09:32,780
Free passage through the Strait.

1000
01:09:33,060 --> 01:09:34,700
We have to deal with the nuclear issue.

1001
01:09:34,700 --> 01:09:38,700
I worry that we've actually lost ground on this problem.

1002
01:09:39,720 --> 01:09:42,540
Because I don't want to see Iran get a nuclear weapon.

1003
01:09:42,620 --> 01:09:44,780
I don't think anybody wants to see that.

1004
01:09:45,200 --> 01:09:49,300
And I think we were on track to preventing that through the nuclear deal we had.

1005
01:09:49,780 --> 01:10:01,500
So trying to get back to that diplomacy we had before this invasion, I think that's probably going to be the ultimate objectives for how to salvage this situation.

1006
01:10:01,500 --> 01:10:04,280
But it is a – I mean, it is a mess from top to bottom.

1007
01:10:04,560 --> 01:10:08,280
And, you know, I really – I came of age after 9-11.

1008
01:10:08,600 --> 01:10:09,520
I was 12 years old.

1009
01:10:09,720 --> 01:10:10,280
9-11 happened.

1010
01:10:11,240 --> 01:10:14,760
And so I understand the importance of keeping this country safe.

1011
01:10:15,580 --> 01:10:24,440
I also saw as a millennial how military disasters like the Iraq war robbed this nation of young lives, of billions of dollars, and of our moral standing in the world.

1012
01:10:24,700 --> 01:10:31,120
I can't believe that we are making the same mistakes just 20 years later in Iran.

1013
01:10:31,120 --> 01:10:37,780
The proposal there sounds a little bit like the memorandum of understanding that was pitched to Iran that ultimately failed.

1014
01:10:38,600 --> 01:10:39,120
You know, free passage.

1015
01:10:39,720 --> 01:10:40,900
Free passage of the Strait of Hormuz.

1016
01:10:41,040 --> 01:10:45,180
There was a clause in there about nuclear weapons and a few other things.

1017
01:10:45,240 --> 01:10:46,820
And it failed within a couple of days.

1018
01:10:47,500 --> 01:10:54,500
So, you know, because I think Iran are playing to a different time frame than Trump is able to considering this sort of four-year election cycle.

1019
01:10:54,840 --> 01:11:00,920
It appears to me – I don't really know what I'm talking about – but it appears to me that Iran are saying, you know, we have the cards.

1020
01:11:01,600 --> 01:11:03,240
You can drop as many bombs as you like.

1021
01:11:03,340 --> 01:11:05,120
We're decentralized in our nature.

1022
01:11:06,080 --> 01:11:10,540
They're split into lots of these smaller factions that are weaponized in their own way.

1023
01:11:10,660 --> 01:11:19,500
There's not particular – I remember one press conference, I think it was Marco Rubio or Pete Hegseth saying that they're not even necessarily sure who is in charge in Iran.

1024
01:11:19,640 --> 01:11:19,720
Yeah.

1025
01:11:19,880 --> 01:11:30,600
And he made a remark that it takes a while to get the carrier pigeons down to the front lines, i.e. the people they're negotiating with might not even be connected with the people that are launching the missiles and the drones, etc.

1026
01:11:30,940 --> 01:11:31,160
Right.

1027
01:11:31,360 --> 01:11:32,600
It just appears to be a total mess.

1028
01:11:33,340 --> 01:11:34,320
And I just can't –

1029
01:11:34,320 --> 01:11:37,420
We do have – but I do believe that we still have economic leverage.

1030
01:11:37,980 --> 01:11:39,600
And there are still economic cards to play.

1031
01:11:39,700 --> 01:11:48,560
And so the key is, you know, I really don't want this disastrous war to play on for the rest of the Trump administration.

1032
01:11:48,920 --> 01:11:53,540
I pray that we can come to a resolution and a conclusion sooner than that.

1033
01:11:53,860 --> 01:12:06,920
And I think that's going to have to involve some of this economic pressure in addition to the regional partnerships because we're ultimately going to have to rely on regional partners in order to actually push through a final resolution.

1034
01:12:07,420 --> 01:12:09,640
So I'm not giving up hope that we can resolve.

1035
01:12:09,700 --> 01:12:10,320
We have to.

1036
01:12:10,440 --> 01:12:12,700
I mean, we just lost one of our own.

1037
01:12:12,860 --> 01:12:18,880
Our fellow Texan, Isabella Gonzalez, lost her life because of an Iranian airstrike in Jordan.

1038
01:12:19,280 --> 01:12:20,460
And she was 19 years old.

1039
01:12:21,120 --> 01:12:24,120
She had just graduated from Hebron High School in Dallas, Texas.

1040
01:12:24,960 --> 01:12:39,680
And the fact that we would send brave members of the armed services like Isabella off to die in another forever war without any exit strategy, without any clear military objective, to me is deep.

1041
01:12:39,700 --> 01:12:40,240
It's deeply immoral.

1042
01:12:41,280 --> 01:12:43,800
Isabella Gonzalez died a patriot.

1043
01:12:43,940 --> 01:12:44,700
She died a hero.

1044
01:12:44,960 --> 01:12:46,800
And we are grateful for her sacrifice.

1045
01:12:47,040 --> 01:12:51,660
But our gratitude for her sacrifice should not be mistaken for an acceptance of her death.

1046
01:12:53,200 --> 01:13:00,300
In terms of what one can do about it, is there any specifics that you have in mind for a way out of this?

1047
01:13:00,360 --> 01:13:02,800
Because I really, I mean, I'm in search of them.

1048
01:13:03,240 --> 01:13:09,680
Well, again, this is why you need checks and balances, because we need the legislative branch to start exerting pressure on the government.

1049
01:13:09,680 --> 01:13:13,400
We need the executive branch so that we can start to see what the objectives are.

1050
01:13:13,520 --> 01:13:16,560
We can come to a consensus about an exit strategy.

1051
01:13:16,760 --> 01:13:18,060
The U.S. can't afford to lose here.

1052
01:13:18,680 --> 01:13:19,040
Agreed.

1053
01:13:19,360 --> 01:13:20,140
Yeah, agreed.

1054
01:13:20,180 --> 01:13:22,140
They can't even afford to be seen as losing.

1055
01:13:22,280 --> 01:13:22,520
That's right.

1056
01:13:22,600 --> 01:13:24,700
Because you're going to embolden the Middle East.

1057
01:13:24,780 --> 01:13:25,080
That's right.

1058
01:13:25,560 --> 01:13:26,780
And Iran is an adversary.

1059
01:13:27,060 --> 01:13:28,080
We should not lose sight of that.

1060
01:13:28,640 --> 01:13:32,900
A nuclear Iran would be a disaster for the region and for the world.

1061
01:13:33,240 --> 01:13:35,100
So it's imperative that we win.

1062
01:13:35,180 --> 01:13:39,060
If you had a phone call with Trump and you had an opportunity to, if he said, listen, James, I need some advice.

1063
01:13:39,060 --> 01:13:39,940
I need some advice.

1064
01:13:40,180 --> 01:13:41,520
This Iran situation has gone out of hand.

1065
01:13:41,600 --> 01:13:42,740
I thought it would be like a Venezuela.

1066
01:13:42,920 --> 01:13:43,860
I thought I'd drop the bombs.

1067
01:13:44,180 --> 01:13:46,760
There'd be an uprising and then it would sort of correct itself.

1068
01:13:46,860 --> 01:13:47,700
But it's gone out of hand.

1069
01:13:47,860 --> 01:13:49,060
James, what advice have you got for me?

1070
01:13:50,140 --> 01:13:50,840
That'd be tough.

1071
01:13:50,840 --> 01:14:01,200
You know, I think that humility is good and that self-awareness is good and hopefully can help him and the administration avoid future mistakes.

1072
01:14:01,480 --> 01:14:08,180
I would urge him to call the leadership in the legislative branch together.

1073
01:14:08,740 --> 01:14:09,020
Leadership.

1074
01:14:09,060 --> 01:14:21,940
And I think that's what we're trying to do is to work with the leadership of both parties, Democrats and Republicans, and work with them as well as our allies around the world to try to come to a consensus on a strategic direction.

1075
01:14:22,180 --> 01:14:30,840
The president and his administration have not only pushed away Congress, they've also done everything they can to alienate our allies across across the globe.

1076
01:14:30,840 --> 01:14:38,940
And there's a reason that this country spent so much political and economic capital on these on these partnerships and these allies.

1077
01:14:38,940 --> 01:14:44,760
And so I would urge the president to start building that big coalition.

1078
01:14:45,060 --> 01:14:48,120
I would I would tell him, frankly, that he's not going to get out of this on his own.

1079
