1138
01:18:37,700 --> 01:18:41,880
And he tells the story of a man who is beaten and left for dead on the side of the road.

1139
01:18:42,680 --> 01:18:44,660
A priest walks by and does nothing.

1140
01:18:45,500 --> 01:18:47,680
A religious man walks by and does nothing.

1141
01:18:48,220 --> 01:18:49,780
And then a Samaritan walks by.

1142
01:18:50,280 --> 01:18:54,760
And, you know, when we hear Samaritan in our modern context, it doesn't really mean a whole lot.

1143
01:18:54,840 --> 01:18:56,900
Most people associate it with helping people on the side of the road.

1144
01:18:57,460 --> 01:19:03,640
But in Jesus's time and for Jesus's listeners, the Samaritans were not just a different religious group.

1145
01:19:04,140 --> 01:19:06,520
They were their sworn enemy.

1146
01:19:07,700 --> 01:19:19,260
And so in Jesus's story, the Samaritan is the one who stops and bandages the man's wounds, nurses him back to health, takes him to an inn, pays the innkeeper to look after him until he comes back.

1147
01:19:20,360 --> 01:19:29,520
And in Jesus's story, the Samaritan is the ultimate, ultimate example of loving thy neighbor as thyself.

1148
01:19:29,880 --> 01:19:32,520
And it's such a, it would have been a shocking story.

1149
01:19:32,640 --> 01:19:35,720
I'm a Texas Longhorn football fan.

