1202
01:22:55,260 --> 01:22:58,320
And I just want to, I am not saying that as a perfect person, I'm really not.

1203
01:22:58,660 --> 01:23:05,940
What I'm trying to do is, is be honest about the clear choice in this race, um, and about the ramifications of electing.

1204
01:23:06,280 --> 01:23:08,700
Someone with Ken Paxton's track record to the U.S. Senate.

1205
01:23:09,520 --> 01:23:11,940
At 31 years old, you went off to, um, Bible school.

1206
01:23:12,300 --> 01:23:12,540
Yeah.

1207
01:23:12,820 --> 01:23:19,320
You know, I had always, um, I looked up to my grandfather, still do, looked up to my pastor, still do.

1208
01:23:19,680 --> 01:23:26,940
And I, you know, always kind of harbored this, this wish to one day preach and, and lead a church.

1209
01:23:27,040 --> 01:23:35,880
Um, I saw how my granddad and my pastor, I just really, I saw how they, how they helped people, how they help people make sense of.

1210
01:23:36,040 --> 01:23:41,280
Their own lives, uh, how they helped people ask some of the deepest questions.

1211
01:23:41,820 --> 01:23:42,540
Who am I?

1212
01:23:42,940 --> 01:23:44,340
What does it mean to be a human being?

1213
01:23:44,440 --> 01:23:45,700
What is the purpose of life?

1214
01:23:46,080 --> 01:23:54,300
I saw them doing that on a, not on a, on a big podcast, but you know, at a small neighborhood church in like a very intimate way with people.

1215
01:23:54,780 --> 01:23:59,400
I saw them comfort people who were grieving in our community.

1216
01:23:59,400 --> 01:24:05,500
When someone loses a loved one, when someone is dying themselves, like their first call is to.

1217
01:24:05,940 --> 01:24:06,220
Dr.

1218
01:24:06,220 --> 01:24:07,200
Jim, my pastor.

1219
01:24:07,740 --> 01:24:11,440
So I always had this idea that I may want to go into that line of work one day.

1220
01:24:12,360 --> 01:24:16,660
And so I was in my second term in the state house and my first term had been great.

1221
01:24:16,740 --> 01:24:22,920
It was, um, 2019, uh, Democrats had flipped all these districts.

1222
01:24:22,920 --> 01:24:26,400
There was a close competitive election statewide that year in 2018.

1223
01:24:26,860 --> 01:24:35,040
So the very next session in 2019, suddenly the Republican majority was ready to work on a bipartisan basis with us Democrats.

1224
01:24:35,520 --> 01:24:35,920
And it was great.

1225
01:24:35,920 --> 01:24:44,620
Because we passed this historic school finance reform package that invested a lot of new money into our schools and we provided property tax relief to working people.

1226
01:24:44,620 --> 01:24:45,280
It was awesome.

1227
01:24:45,680 --> 01:24:47,080
And I was like, I have the best job in the world.

1228
01:24:47,920 --> 01:24:50,800
Fast forward to the next session in 2021.

1229
01:24:51,480 --> 01:24:57,700
And it was one of the most partisan, one of the most unproductive legislative sessions in modern Texas history.

1230
01:24:57,800 --> 01:25:01,740
That's when you saw the most extreme abortion ban in the country go into effect.

1231
01:25:02,040 --> 01:25:05,900
You saw permitless carry, um, one of these crazy gun laws that allows.

1232
01:25:05,920 --> 01:25:10,660
People to, to carry these weapons without any kind of background check, without any kind of license.

1233
01:25:11,040 --> 01:25:17,080
You saw efforts to ban books and whitewash curriculum, voter suppression, trying to make it harder for people to vote in Texas.

1234
01:25:17,080 --> 01:25:19,420
Texas is one of the hardest places to vote in the country.

1235
01:25:20,080 --> 01:25:22,360
So it was a really difficult session.

1236
01:25:22,880 --> 01:25:29,980
And I just started to doubt that I was really making an impact that I was really doing what I was supposed to do.

1237
01:25:30,980 --> 01:25:35,560
And after meeting with my pastor about it, sitting down with my granddad about it.

1238
01:25:35,920 --> 01:25:42,340
Both of them reminded me that Jesus gave us these two commandments for a reason, right?

1239
01:25:42,360 --> 01:25:50,500
I had been so focused on that second commandment to love my neighbor in the classroom, in the halls of power, in serving my community.

1240
01:25:51,220 --> 01:26:00,540
That despite going to church, you know, on Sunday, I really wasn't giving enough attention and time to that first commandment to love God.

1241
01:26:00,960 --> 01:26:05,900
To love that, that sacred something that we are all an expression of.

1242
01:26:05,920 --> 01:26:30,260
And so I made the decision to, to go to seminary, to follow that first commandment and what I've discovered, because now I'm a seminarian and a lawmaker at the same time, and I've realized that these two commandments, again, given to us as a whole by Jesus, those two commandments, they sustain, they enrich, they challenge each other.

1243
01:26:30,260 --> 01:26:31,720
They're in relationship.

1244
01:26:32,420 --> 01:26:35,760
And at least for me, personally speaking, it helped balance me out.

1245
01:26:36,080 --> 01:26:47,140
Because if I'm being honest, if you're devoting all of your energy to loving your neighbor, especially your neighbors who, who you disagree with, it's really easy to burn out.

1246
01:26:47,460 --> 01:26:51,140
And I think anyone who does, you know, service work can tell you that.

1247
01:26:51,540 --> 01:26:59,620
And it's why we have to be in touch with the ground of our own being, the God that all of us express every day.

1248
01:27:00,820 --> 01:27:04,640
To me, you need both the inner life and the outer life.

1249
01:27:04,640 --> 01:27:05,900
You need prayer and action.

1250
01:27:06,160 --> 01:27:09,680
Those two things go hand in hand and without it, you burn out.

1251
01:27:10,460 --> 01:27:18,780
Or if you're only focused on loving God and not loving our neighbors, which a lot of people do that too, you become self-centered, you become disconnected from the world.

1252
01:27:19,340 --> 01:27:23,460
And so going to seminary was my way of trying to unite these two commandments in my own life.

1253
01:27:24,120 --> 01:27:28,600
And honestly, it was, I think it's the best decision I've ever made to go to seminary.

1254
01:27:28,600 --> 01:27:31,840
Where does your faith in a God, in Jesus come from?

1255
01:27:32,620 --> 01:27:35,360
Like what is the, you know, a faith is probably kind of,

1256
01:27:35,920 --> 01:27:44,160
maybe the answer to the question, but what makes you believe that there is a higher power, that Jesus is who he pledged to be back all those years ago?

1257
01:27:44,800 --> 01:27:45,300
Yeah.

1258
01:27:45,300 --> 01:27:56,700
As a seminary student, I had to learn Greek and Hebrew because in our Christian Bible, the Hebrew scriptures are written in Hebrew and the New Testament is written in Greek.

1259
01:27:57,160 --> 01:28:02,660
And so we learned both original languages so that we don't have to be reliant on translations, right?

1260
01:28:02,660 --> 01:28:04,040
Because things get lost in translation.

1261
01:28:04,040 --> 01:28:21,200
And so I was studying this word, faith, and I realized that it can be translated both as belief, which is how it's often translated, you know, a commitment to some kind of value, some kind of concept, an idea, right?

1262
01:28:21,220 --> 01:28:25,240
That's a belief, but it can also be translated as trust.

1263
01:28:26,880 --> 01:28:31,620
And suddenly, you know, this was like my first year of seminary and I was already having like a light bulb moment.

1264
01:28:32,400 --> 01:28:33,960
And I realized like, that's how my faith.

1265
01:28:34,040 --> 01:28:35,160
Faith feels to me.

1266
01:28:35,560 --> 01:28:38,780
It feels much more like trust than it does belief.

1267
01:28:39,120 --> 01:28:45,740
It's not a commitment to some abstract concept, some intellectual idea.

1268
01:28:46,360 --> 01:28:53,840
It is a visceral bodily experience of almost like relaxing.

1269
01:28:54,860 --> 01:28:59,440
The best analogy I can think of, because when we talk about God, we're always talking in analogies.

1270
01:29:00,500 --> 01:29:03,020
I learned to swim at the Wells Branch pool.

1271
01:29:04,040 --> 01:29:05,860
In the neighborhood where I grew up.

1272
01:29:06,220 --> 01:29:12,140
And I remember when I was learning how to swim, my swim instructor, she said, she told me, don't fight the water.

1273
01:29:13,200 --> 01:29:14,540
Don't hold on to the water.

1274
01:29:14,920 --> 01:29:16,540
Let the water hold on to you.

1275
01:29:17,540 --> 01:29:24,760
And it was a big moment for me in learning how to swim, but that's exactly how my faith feels.

1276
01:29:25,240 --> 01:29:28,900
So much of our lives is trying to control our surroundings, right?

1277
01:29:29,240 --> 01:29:30,280
Trying to control the people.

1278
01:29:30,660 --> 01:29:34,020
And when you realize that we are an expression of something much deeper.

1279
01:29:34,360 --> 01:29:35,060
And much bigger.

1280
01:29:35,760 --> 01:29:39,040
You're suddenly able to let that hold you instead of trying to hold on to it.

1281
01:29:39,320 --> 01:29:42,360
And why this religion versus all the other available religions?

1282
01:29:42,360 --> 01:29:43,700
Why are you trusting in this one?

1283
01:29:44,000 --> 01:29:52,220
We all inherit a religious tradition and it is an inheritance that's passed on from one generation to the next.

1284
01:29:52,460 --> 01:29:53,420
And it's such a gift.

1285
01:29:53,900 --> 01:29:59,240
It's such a gift that, that I feel like a lot of people nowadays are abandoning.

1286
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:04,460
For understandable reasons. I mean, we have seen religious corruption in our religious

1287
01:30:04,460 --> 01:30:08,900
institutions across this country, across the world, including my home state of Texas. We've

1288
01:30:08,900 --> 01:30:16,620
seen religious leaders abuse children. We've seen them lie. We've seen them amass wealth and power

